WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.020
The best way to prevent leaks is often to avoid manual memory management in application code.

2
00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:14.680
If an object can be owned directly as a local value, member value, or returned value, that ownership is usually clearer than dynamic allocation.

3
00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:18.060
The lifetime follows scope or containing object lifetime.

4
00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:23.620
Use standard containers instead of manually allocated dynamic structures where practical.

5
00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:36.200
std::vector, std::string, std::map, std::unordered_map, and other standard library types manage storage and cleanup for their elements.

6
00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:42.060
They also keep size, ownership, and resource behavior closer to the data.

7
00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:45.940
RAII is the central C++ cleanup principle.

8
00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:52.040
A resource is acquired or represented by an object, and the object's destructor releases that resource.

9
00:00:52.140 --> 00:01:00.540
This works across normal execution, early returns, exceptions according to project policy, and partial failures during construction.

10
00:01:00.820 --> 00:01:05.140
Destructors make cleanup consistent because the cleanup rule lives with the owning object.

11
00:01:05.240 --> 00:01:09.320
A caller does not have to remember every branch that needs a release operation.

12
00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:14.700
Reviewers can inspect the owner type and its move or copy behavior to understand lifecycle.

13
00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:18.180
Simpler ownership is easier to test and maintain.

14
00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:23.080
Values and containers usually reduce the number of explicit transfer points.

15
00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:27.420
RAII wrappers concentrate cleanup logic in one place.

16
00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:32.800
Smart pointers can then be reserved for cases where dynamic ownership is genuinely needed.

17
00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:36.200
This approach also improves change safety.

18
00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:47.900
When maintenance work adds a new return path, validation branch, or failure condition, RAII and container ownership keep cleanup behavior tied to scope instead of scattered manual calls.
